course: dictionary
lesson: 7592
title: Prickly: one word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the sentence tells you which one to use. Prickly can mean covered in sharp points, like a hedgehog. «A hedgehog has about 5,000 to 7,000 spines and rolls into a tight prickly ball when frightened.» Here the spines are the clue, so prickly means covered in sharp points.
explain: Prickly can also describe a feeling or a person. «He pulled on the scratchy wool and complained that his prickly jumper made his arms tingle» tells you it is about a stinging feeling on the skin. In a story, a prickly character is easily annoyed or upset, so grumpy. Ask yourself which meaning fits the clues around the word.
explain: Always check your meaning by putting it back into the sentence. If you replace prickly with grumpy in a sentence about a spiky ball, it makes no sense, so you know to choose a different meaning. Good readers try the meanings until one fits.
fact: Prickly means covered in sharp points or prickles.
fact: «Prickly» can also mean giving a stinging or tingling feeling, like a prickly jumper.
fact: For a person, «prickly» means easily annoyed or upset, or grumpy.
fact: «Prickly heat» is an itchy rash in hot weather, and «a prickly subject» is a problem that is hard to deal with and likely to cause arguments.
fact: Prickly plants such as cactus, gorse, holly and thistles use prickles to protect themselves from being eaten.
exercise: «A hedgehog rolls into a tight prickly ball when it is frightened.» What does prickly mean here? | Very happy and playful | Wet through from rain | Soft and warm to touch | *Covered in sharp points
exercise: «His prickly jumper made his arms tingle all day.» What does prickly mean here? | *Stinging or tingling against the skin | Very long and loose | Bright and shiny | Soft and silky
exercise: Maya says, «Grandad is being prickly today.» What does she mean? | *Grumpy and easily annoyed | Full of jokes and laughter | Covered in spines like a cactus | Sleepy after a big lunch
test: «Her teacher said the topic was a prickly subject, because everyone kept arguing about it.» What does prickly mean here? | Easy and fun to talk about | Bright and colourful | *Hard to deal with and likely to cause arguments | Covered in sharp points
test: Which of these is a prickly plant? | Tortoise | Snail | *Holly | Lettuce
exam: A teacher says, «Don't be so prickly this morning, you were fine yesterday.» What does prickly mean here? | Hard to lift | Covered in spines | Calm and cheerful | *Grumpy and easily upset
